From: Kali NetHunter rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: rhodep: drive the camera flash via PM6125 PWM

The rear flash lit up and died after ~1s because it was wired as a plain
gpio-led on tlmm 49 (patch 0092). tlmm 49 is only the analog flash driver
IC's enable; the sustained current comes from a PWM current source on
PM6125 GPIO8 (func1), fed by the PM6125 LPG/PWM at SPMI 0xb300. There is no
flash regulator (the IC hangs off VBAT), so lighting it needs the enable
held asserted AND the PWM running.

Two findings from bring-up drove the final shape:
  - the enable must be actively held low the whole time -- a one-shot
    gpiod_set_value (what pwm-leds' enable-gpios does) lets the LED die a
    second later, while a persistently requested gpio keeps it lit;
  - the PWM alone (with the enable held) toggles the LED cleanly on/off.

So pwm-leds here owns only the PWM (channel 0, period 50000 ns / 20 kHz,
the value the vendor hardcodes; the LPG rounds it to ~39 us), which is what
KDE Plasma's flashlight button drives -- it writes max_brightness to
/sys/class/leds/*:torch. The tlmm 49 enable is held low for the session by
the rhodep-flashlight service (packages/rhodep-flashlight) rather than by
this node. Add the pwm@b300 node under the PM6125's second SPMI USID
(pmic@1, left empty by mainline pm6125.dtsi) and route the PWM to the PMIC
GPIO8 pad via its func1 pinctrl (applied by the driver core on the pwm@b300
device, since the LPG driver does not touch pinctrl). Depends on the
companion patch adding qcom,pm6125-pwm to leds-qcom-lpg.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts	2026-09-23 17:26:18.520849006 +0000
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts	2026-09-23 18:46:07.865812013 +0000
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
 #include <dt-bindings/interconnect/qcom,sm6375.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/input/input.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/leds/common.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/pinctrl/qcom,pmic-gpio.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/power/qcom-rpmpd.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/clock/qcom,dsi-phy-28nm.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/clock/qcom,sm6375-dispcc.h>
@@ -314,22 +315,26 @@
 	};
 
 	/*
-	 * The rear camera flash. The vendor drives it as a plain GPIO --
-	 * flash-type = <CAM_FLASH_TYPE_GPIO> on tlmm 49 -- rather than through
-	 * an i2c LED controller, so in mainline terms it is a gpio-led and
-	 * nothing more. Confirmed on the device before this node existed:
-	 * tlmm 49 was unclaimed, and driving it blinks the LED.
+	 * The rear camera flash. It is NOT a plain enable-GPIO LED: the vendor
+	 * drives it as a PWM current source on PM6125 GPIO8 (func1), fed by the
+	 * PM6125 single-channel LPG/PWM at SPMI 0xb300, plus an enable on tlmm 49
+	 * that the analog flash driver IC needs held asserted the whole time it
+	 * is lit (a static level is not enough -- the pin must stay actively
+	 * requested low, which pwm-leds' one-shot gpiod_set_value does not do, so
+	 * the LED died a second after lighting). The rhodep-flashlight service
+	 * holds tlmm 49 low for the duration; pwm-leds here only owns the PWM,
+	 * so KDE Plasma's flashlight button (which writes max_brightness to
+	 * /sys/class/leds/*:torch) toggles the current cleanly. period is the
+	 * 50000 ns / 20 kHz the vendor hardcodes (the LPG rounds it to ~39 us).
 	 *
-	 * Exposed as a torch rather than a camera flash on purpose. A strobe
-	 * synchronised to an exposure needs the v4l2-flash class and a camera
-	 * that captures, and neither exists here yet; a flashlight only needs
-	 * to turn on and stay on.
+	 * Exposed as a torch (a flashlight only has to turn on and stay on).
 	 */
-	leds {
-		compatible = "gpio-leds";
+	pwmleds {
+		compatible = "pwm-leds";
 
 		led-torch {
-			gpios = <&tlmm 49 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+			pwms = <&pm6125_pwm 0 50000>;	/* 20 kHz, from the vendor */
+			max-brightness = <255>;
 			function = LED_FUNCTION_TORCH;
 			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_WHITE>;
 			default-state = "off";
@@ -1925,3 +1930,36 @@
 		};
 	};
 };
+
+/*
+ * Camera flash PWM current source. The PM6125's single-channel LPG/PWM
+ * block lives at SPMI 0xb300 on the second SPMI USID (pmic@1); mainline's
+ * pm6125.dtsi leaves that node empty. qcom,pm6125-pwm is added to the
+ * leds-qcom-lpg driver in the companion patch. Its output only reaches the
+ * PMIC GPIO8 pad once that pad is muxed to "func1"; the LPG driver does not
+ * touch pinctrl, so the pinctrl-0 here is applied by the driver core when
+ * the pwm@b300 device is bound.
+ */
+&spmi_bus {
+	pmic@1 {
+		pm6125_pwm: pwm@b300 {
+			compatible = "qcom,pm6125-pwm";
+			reg = <0xb300>;
+			#pwm-cells = <2>;
+			pinctrl-names = "default";
+			pinctrl-0 = <&camera_flash_pwm_default>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&pm6125_gpios {
+	camera_flash_pwm_default: camera-flash-pwm-default-state {
+		pins = "gpio8";
+		function = "func1";		/* PWM alternate function */
+		input-disable;
+		output-low;
+		bias-disable;
+		power-source = <0>;
+		qcom,drive-strength = <PMIC_GPIO_STRENGTH_LOW>;
+	};
+};
